# The filter commands are interactive, so drive the REPL over stdin. This pins
# the output format the reader actually sees; the unit tests cover the matching
# rules underneath.

# RUN: printf 'matches\nfilter kind exercise\nmatches\nfilter\nfilter template Nonexistent\nq\n' | %dpm open %root/tests/fixtures/compare/trace-b.json --visualize --color never | FileCheck %s

# The startup banner has to advertise the commands: a reader who never types
# `help` learns them only from here.
# CHECK: search: filter <value>, find <value>, matches

# `matches` before any filter explains itself rather than printing an empty list.
# CHECK: no filter set

# Applying a filter reports what it selected out of how many, so a reader can
# tell "one hit" from "no hits" without walking the trace.
# CHECK: filter: kind exercise  (1 of 2 steps)

# CHECK: matches: kind exercise
# The step number and the event id are both shown: [step] is what `j`
# takes, the id is what `collapse` and the tree use.
# CHECK: > [1] #2:0 EXERCISE Token:Token.Transfer

# CHECK: filter cleared

# A filter selecting nothing is refused, not applied -- applying it would strand
# navigation with every step excluded and no way to see why.
# CHECK: no events match template Nonexistent; filter not applied
